<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="note"><b class="h">Claim risks around Ruidoso</b><ul class="chips-row"><li>Wildfire</li><li>Post-fire flash flooding</li><li>Smoke damage</li><li>Snow load</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></div>

<h2>A mountain village tested by fire and water</h2>
<p>Ruidoso sits in the pine forests of the Sacramento Mountains in Lincoln County, a cool retreat from the desert below and a popular destination for second homes and vacation rentals. That forested setting carries real wildfire risk. In June 2024, the South Fork and Salt fires burned through the area, forcing the evacuation of the village and destroying many homes and other structures. Earlier, the Little Bear Fire in 2012 had also burned homes in the region.</p>
<p>The fires were only the beginning. Burn scars on the slopes above the village dramatically increased runoff, and monsoon storms in the seasons that followed produced repeated flash flooding along the Rio Ruidoso and its tributaries, including deadly flooding in 2025. For many owners, a fire claim was followed by a separate flood loss, each governed by different coverage.</p>
<h2>Housing in Ruidoso</h2>
<p>Ruidoso’s homes include cabins and timber-frame houses tucked among the pines, log homes, and larger custom homes on hillsides. Pitched metal roofs are common for snow shedding and fire resistance. The <strong>Upper Canyon</strong> holds older cabins close to the river, many used as vacation rentals. <strong>Alto</strong>, north of the village, features larger homes and golf course properties. <strong>Ruidoso Downs</strong>, a separate city to the east, is home to the racetrack and a mix of homes and manufactured housing. Many properties are second homes, which affects how losses are discovered and documented.</p>
<h2>Claim disputes common in Lincoln County</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total loss valuation.</strong> Rebuilding a custom mountain home on a steep lot costs more than many estimating tools assume, especially when contractors are in short supply after a disaster.</li>
<li><strong>Contents inventories.</strong> For second homes and rentals, owners may not remember every item, and valuation disputes are common.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke damage.</strong> Homes that survived the fires may still need extensive cleaning, HVAC work and contents treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Fire versus flood.</strong> When a fire-damaged property is later flooded, separating the two losses for coverage purposes can be complicated.</li>
<li><strong>Short-term rental income.</strong> Loss of rental income depends on the policy and how the property is insured.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/appraiser-services/wildfire-loss-appraisal/">wildfire loss appraisal</a> and <a href="/appraiser-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">fire and smoke damage appraisal</a> pages explain how we approach these claims.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Fire and flood are separate</b>Standard homeowners policies generally cover wildfire but exclude flood and mudflow. Flood coverage is purchased separately, often through the NFIP, and usually has a waiting period before it takes effect.</div>
<h2>How we help in Ruidoso</h2>
<p>We serve Ruidoso, Alto and Ruidoso Downs from our statewide practice, without a local office. We review your policy, the carrier’s valuation and any inventories you’ve prepared, then inspect the property and build an independent estimate. Our <a href="/claim-estimators/wildfire-contents-inventory-estimator/">wildfire contents inventory estimator</a> can help you organize a contents list.</p>
<p>If you and the carrier can’t agree on value, we can be appointed as your appraiser. Read <a href="/inside-the-appraisal-process/">inside the appraisal process</a>, see nearby <a href="/cities-we-serve/alamogordo/">Alamogordo</a>, or <a href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Services available in Ruidoso</h2>
<p>Every New Mexico Insurance Appraiser service is available in Ruidoso and the rest of Lincoln County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraiser-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/hail-roof-appraisal/">Hail &amp; Roof Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/high-wind-damage-appraisal/">High Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraiser-services/wildfire-loss-appraisal/">Wildfire Loss Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Ruidoso claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My Ruidoso home was damaged by the 2024 fires and then by flooding. How are those handled?</summary><div class="a"><p>They are usually treated as separate losses under different coverage. Wildfire damage generally falls under your homeowners policy, while flood damage requires separate flood insurance, so careful documentation of each event matters.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal help with a wildfire contents claim?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. If the disagreement is about the value of contents, appraisal can address it. A detailed inventory with descriptions, ages and replacement costs strengthens your position.</p></div></details><details><summary>I own a vacation rental in the Upper Canyon. Can you help even though I don’t live there?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We serve properties statewide and can coordinate inspections with property managers or caretakers.</p></div></details></div>
